DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I hear that the brain blocks traumatic memories. Why does it do this?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Hm... It’s probably to prevent us from being driven crazy by constantly remembering bad things.  It sounds like the human brain makes up a story or hypothesis about what happened in a way that makes sense and feels safe, then fills in the details of the story later with vague but accurate memories.  The brain then can edit the story with new information and adjust as it gets older and experiences more.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Wow, it really tries to protect us huh'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yeah, a lot of what we perceive is just a useful story that the brain creates.  Sometimes it makes up a story so quickly, we’re not even aware of it.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'And where does it store these traumatic memories? Where in the brain?'}]
