DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Hi, I need to do a school report about the U.S. election system. Can you explain how presidential elections work in the United States?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I can! Let’s start at the beginning. The United States has the Electoral College. This is a system of distributing votes for president by state, rather than giving all votes to the winner in each state.  Here’s how it works: there are 538 electoral votes.  Each state has a number of electors equal to the number of their representatives in the House plus 2 electors for each of their senators.  The number of electors each state has is proportional to their population (as reported in the census every ten years).  \nBecause states are grouped into “electoral districts”, each state is usually allocated a number of electoral votes equal to the number of its electors, unless there are less than five electors in a state, in which case they will have an extra electoral vote.  The electors who represent a state at the Electoral College, then cast votes for president.  The state’s electors are bound by the state’s laws to vote for whichever presidential candidate wins the popular vote in the state.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yeah, that helps a lot. After voting occurs, how are the votes used to to determine a winner?'}]
