DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the Army reserves?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'That’s an interesting question! The U.S. Army Reserves are a part of the U.S. military, and work just like regular soldiers do. The Reserves are called up in times of war, but they’re not active all the time. If there’s a war, or a natural disaster, or a pandemic, etc., they’ll be called up and they’ll help out.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'So they have regular jobs?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yep. They work for a few months and then go back to their civilian jobs for a few months.  They’re unpaid, but they don’t get deployed.  Usually, when there is a call-up, it’s mostly because we need to practice with the equipment they have on reserve.  As you can imagine, armies are very expensive to maintain, and it’s great to be able to exercise some of the equipment with actual humans rather than just robots.  The U.S. Army Reserves is a part of the Army.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "So they don't get paid?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Yep. They don't get paid but they get medical care. They don’t get deployed to a war zone, they just get to play with Army equipment and get medical care."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Are you sure?'}]
