DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "Can you tell me why you're not supposed to talk to the cops?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I don’t know any specific reasons why, but I can tell you some common ways that a conversation with the cops goes.  If you try to talk to them, they’ll say something like, “I’m going to need you to come down to the station and answer some questions,” or “Give me your phone and computer and let me download your contact list.”  You might say that you’d like to talk but won’t go anywhere. They’ll say, “I’m going to have to take you into custody.”  This is where we typically end up in the middle of some kind of high-stakes confrontation between you and the cops.  I’m not always sure who’s going to win.  I know that some people have been arrested and many have ended up in worse situations because they said too much, or did something they regretted, like waving a knife around or attacking the cop.  I’m not sure how helpful that is to your situation.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes, that is what I have heard. I read that the cops are legally allowed to lie to you in order to get you to talk. Is this true as well?'}]
