DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is Multiple Sclerosis?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I have some understanding of the disease, which is mostly a decline in the function of the nerves in the body.  A person with MS is likely to have reduced strength or coordination of limbs, a sensation of numbness or tingling in certain parts of the body, visual problems, a stiff neck, dizziness, an imbalance, or even paralysis, and some times speech or other cognitive difficulties.  There is no cure for MS, but there are treatments for the symptoms and side effects.  MS is most common in people who are 30-50 years old.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What kind of treatments are there?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The most commonly used medications for MS are interferon beta, corticosteroids, and monoclonal antibodies, but people can also benefit from non-medication treatment.  Many people get pain relief by using techniques like relaxation, deep breathing, and various forms of physical activity, and some have success using alternative therapies like acupuncture or yoga.  In some cases a doctor can recommend steroid injections, but the treatment can cause serious side effects like mood swings, weight gain, and increased risk of infection.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can they live a normal life?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What should I do to treat a jammed finger?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well, what sort of jammed finger do you mean? There’s a “charley horse” (which is a cramp in a muscle) and a “jammed finger” (where the fingernail sticks into the finger). In either case, one way to remove a stuck fingernail is to grab the nail from the side with tweezers or pliers, and pull it towards you, while also pulling on the skin and nail at the other end. It might be helpful to pull the nail out a little bit, so the whole finger is pulled along with the nail. That should free the nail, and keep you from injuring your finger.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "It's not a cramp and it doesn't have anything to do with a fingernail. A jammed finger is when the end of your finger gets pushed straight back in a forceful way."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well, sometimes it’s helpful to reduce the pain first, by giving yourself an ice pack or rubbing some ice on the tip of your finger. Once the pain is reduced, you can put more pressure on your finger.   You could push on it very firmly with your hand, for example, or you could push on the other end of the finger.  If you give it a quick hard tap with a hammer, that could help as well.  If that doesn’t work, then you can try to increase the pressure in other ways, like squeezing your finger.  Of course you’ll want to avoid doing any permanent damage to the finger.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "An ice pack sounds good and I will want to stabilize it for now. I definitely don't want to use a hammer like you recommended. That sounds painful."}]
