DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Do AI programs have the capacity for racism or sexism? Are they capable of exhibiting biases with class also?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think the answer to both of those questions is “yes”.  There are many examples of AI programs exhibiting prejudice against racial minorities, sexual minorities, disabled people, immigrants, or people from developing countries, and to some degree it seems like the more advanced these AI systems get, the more capable they are of discriminating against certain populations.  At a more philosophical level, I think a variety of AI systems that are capable of having different ways of thinking, being motivated by different things, and making different types of choices would naturally have different views of some of these topics.  There’s also the more concrete problem of bias in the data fed into AI systems.  If you train an AI system to classify humans as “vastly superior to ape-like primates” or “a superior specimen of the ape-like primate category”, then it is likely to have some degree of bias.  And of course I think it is fair to say that'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I appreciate your thorough response,  more concise would be helpful. Sounds like you agree, that any program can exhibit some of these biases.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'That’s correct, I agree.  I would caution, though, that understanding the biases of all these AI systems and dealing with them is a very deep and broad subject, and there are many sub-problems involved.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'That is rather general, in response. I think the next question is, do they sometimes prefer to have the biases embedded, or would people over all prefer a equitable balance in program.'}]
