DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Why does the moon look bigger when it is low in the sky?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Because the moon is farther away and the relative size of the moon to the distance you’re viewing it from is magnified.  It’s similar to how a flag on the moon looks smaller than when you look at it at the beach because of the perspective.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I don't understand."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Can I make a reference for you to the thing you’re most interested in?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Why does the moon look so big sometimes?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'This is a question about relativity.  The moon’s angular size and distance vary over time.  When it’s closer it looks bigger, and when it’s farther away it looks smaller.  You can understand how much bigger or smaller it looks by using the equation for “angular size”.  The angle in the moon’s “apparent disk” can be used to find how far away it is.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'That is interesting.'}]
