DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "How come divers need to depressurize when they don't even dive that far down in the ocean, yet we can fly thousands of feet in the air without issues?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'It’s a good question!  For humans, a major risk of diving is to experience “barotrauma,” which refers to the negative pressure inside the lungs that can result when we breathe in the “lower” atmospheric pressure of the ocean.  Once our lungs are filled with that compressed air, the body doesn’t have any more room to expand and in order to release the pressure, the lungs might actually rupture.  So it’s really important that divers go through a series of steps in which the amount of air inside their lungs gets reduced slowly, because even a small amount of “over-pressure” can cause serious damage.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "So why doesn't flying really high cause any sorts of issues in comparison?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Flying really high is really a complicated situation.  In both cases, the lungs are experiencing a change in pressure relative to the environment around us.  However, in the case of flying, we’re much more buoyant because our body is covered with “flotation devices” like clothing, like a parachute, and even like the wings on a plane.  So that allows the lungs to “float” as well and we experience almost no pressure changes.  The part of your lungs that isn’t filled with air will experience a relatively low pressure, since there’s almost no “outside pressure” at all.  This “negative pressure” will cause the air to expand in your lungs, but because the lungs are wrapped around the body, there’s not much room for that air to expand.  So your lungs can expand about as much as they would in the same situation if you were lying in bed without any clothes on and just breathing normally.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "So basically, the lesser pressure in the skies just doesn't affect us negatively like the greater pressure in the ocean does?"}]
