ennonite community as a symbol of spiritual empowerment. This is a significant aspect of their identity.\n\n', '</think>\n\nTo find out if there are any road closures in your area due to a storm, you can use the following steps and resources:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Check Local Government Websites**\n- **Local government websites** (e.g., city or county government) often post road closure updates after storms.\n- Look for:\n  - **"Road Closure"** or **"Weather Conditions"** sections.\n  - **"Updates"** or **"Emergency"** pages.\n  - **"Weather Impact"** or **"Storm Impact"** alerts.\n\n**Examples:**\n- [City of [Your City]] (e.g., [www.yourcity.gov](https://www.yourcity.gov))\n- [State Department of Transportation](https://www.dot.state.va.us/), if you\'re in Virginia.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Use Emergency Management Services**\n- **Local Emergency Management Agency** (EMAC) will often provide updates.\n- Check:\n  - **Emergency', '</think>\n\nThat’s a great idea! Babysitting 5-year-olds is both rewarding and exciting, and playing games like hide and seek can be a fun and engaging way to keep them entertained. Here are some fun and age-appropriate hide and seek games you can play with your toddlers next week:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Classic Hide and Seek**\n**Goal:** Find a hidden toy or person.\n\n**How to Play:**\n- Choose a toy (like a ball, a toy car, or a stuffed animal) to hide.\n- One child is the "hider," and the rest are the "finders."\n- The hider will hide, and the finders will search for the toy.\n- The first to find the hidden toy wins.\n\n**Variation:**\n- Use a "secret spot" or "hiding place" where the hider can hide. You can use a blanket, a box, or a closet to make it more fun.\n\n---\n\n### **2', "Okay, so I want to become a court reporter. Let me think about how to start. First, I need to understand what a court reporter does. From what I remember, they transcribe spoken words into written text during legal proceedings. They have to be very precise because even 