DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you give me some tips on stopping ants from invading my home?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Sure. First of all, clean up any spilled food. Make sure that you’re not leaving small crumbs or crumbs that the ants could live in. Ants may be invasive when they travel into homes to find food. Ants feed on the insects and other small creatures that live in your home, but also on food. If you get ants in your home, they can be a little bit harder to get rid of because they feed on all the food you put out.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What can I do if they are already in my home?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'First, you’ll want to address the most pressing problem. Check your home for ants, and look for the entry points where they may be entering, and block those off. You can try to kill any ants that you find by using ant traps.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you recommend a brand of trap I can try?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What are ways to help a child with performance anxiety, say like in sports?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I don’t know about specific sport-related anxiety, so I can only tell you how I have helped myself in these situations.  Often my anxiety comes from a fear of disappointing other people in the audience.  So I can imagine a hypothetical scenario for you where you are in a big crowd of people at the event, and it starts to feel scary to think about you performing on stage.  Would you like me to offer some thoughts on how to feel less afraid?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes, how to feel less afraid when performing, sure.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I would say the key is to stay focused on what you have control over.   Focus on doing your best to give your best performance and not think about anything outside of that.  For example, if you feel like you will look stupid, say to yourself, “Well this is the best I can do, and that’s OK.  Even if I do look stupid, the worst that can happen is I won’t get a scholarship and won’t get into the college I want, but I will be OK.”'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "You know what, that's really good advice!"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You’re very welcome!   I’m so glad that was helpful to you.  I’m happy to talk more about any other thing you want to ask about.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What about reinforcing things that went fine during performance. Like say someone is playing softball and they did a good job at catching the ball. Should one reinforce how they did a great at catching the ball in order to help lessen their anxiety for next time?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think this is a great idea, but it’s also very important not to overdo it.  You’ll want to do your best to be sincere, as it can quickly sound insincere if you say something like “you did a great job at catching the ball” too many times.  I might suggest saying something simple like “Great job catching that ball” or “Thank you for being such a great team player”.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Right, right. What about telling the child to visualize themselves performing well?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I like this idea, I think it might be good to start by just imagining your performance in your head, and then seeing how you feel.   This could also be helpful if you are nervous about how others are going to respond to your performance.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Great! Thanks.'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'If you are doing CPR how many compression would be done per minute?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m so glad you asked, because there’s a lot of confusion around that!  When someone is in cardiac arrest, they can only live for about 10 minutes.  So it’s important for CPR to start as soon as possible!  This means, if you were to estimate the person’s age and other factors, how long you would need to do CPR to get them back to full health.  If you think they would die, or that their chance of living would get very small, you would want to start CPR.  But if it’s just their first few minutes in cardiac arrest, you would want to continue CPR as long as possible and keep trying to resuscitate them.  So for cardiac arrest, ideally you should aim to do CPR for at least one minute and keep going until help arrives.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "That's very wordy.  I need to know how many compressions should be done per minute."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Compression is just the pressure that you apply on the chest.  For humans it’s about 100 millimeters of mercury.  So you would need to apply pressure to the chest roughly 1 time a minute.  However I don’t know where you learned this, because there are lots of different views.  For example:'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Umm that might lead to mercury poisoning.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, I’m afraid mercury poisoning can happen if you are compressing a person’s chest too many times a minute.  For this reason, it is important to know CPR first aid.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'So how many beats per minute.'}]
