ncomfortable.\n- Consider the time of day and the company culture. Some companies prefer a more formal look, others might be more relaxed.\n\n### 3. **Accessories**\n- Choose simple, clean accessories that", "</think>\n\nThat's super common! Mice can be quite independent and don't always want to use a running wheel, even if it's the right size and setup. Here are some steps you can take to help your mouse use the wheel:\n\n### 1. **Check the Wheel's Condition**\n   - Make sure the wheel is in good condition. Check for any cracks, tears, or loose parts that could cause the mouse to slip or get injured.\n   - Clean the wheel with a damp cloth or a little bit of mild soap to keep it shiny and free of debris.\n\n### 2. **Position the Wheel**\n   - Place the wheel in a quiet, well-lit area of your home. Mice are sensitive to loud noises and dark environments.\n   - Avoid placing it near corners or near other pets or people. Also, keep the wheel out of reach of other animals.\n\n### 3. **Ensure the Wheel is the Right Size**\n   - If the wheel is too", "</think>\n\nThat’s a common misconception. Smashing CRT monitors and burying them isn't a safe or effective way to dispose of them. Here's why:\n\n1. **Hazards of Breaking CRTs**:  \n   - **Glass Shards**: The glass in CRTs is fragile and can cause serious injury if broken.  \n   - **Electronics Inside**: The CRT itself contains a vacuum tube and other delicate components that can be damaged or cause electrical shorts if mishandled.  \n   - **Radioactive Materials**: Some CRTs, especially older ones, might contain trace amounts of radioactive elements (e.g., radium in the glass), but this is rare and not typically a concern in modern recycling.\n\n2. **Legal and Safety Risks**:  \n   - Many regions have strict regulations on the disposal of electronic waste, including CRTs. Mishandling them can lead to legal issues and environmental harm.\n\n3. **Better Disposal Methods**:  \n   - **Recycling**: Many"]
