DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What are the origins of leprechauns?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Leprechauns were invented by the Irish to help explain the large amounts of gold that were found in their homeland in the mid-18th century.  Gold was rare in the Europe of that time, and they couldn’t explain it scientifically, so they decided to ascribe it to mysterious elves.  The leprechauns were described as a particularly weird type of elf.  “Leprechaun” comes from “Luchruinn”, an Irish word for little body (corruptions of the word “luch”, meaning “body”, and “ruadh”, meaning “little”).  The word “leprechaun” entered the English language in the 18th century.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Are they real?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'They don’t have solid evidence for their existence, so they’re probably not.  They’re a bit like the elves in Tolkien’s books.  They aren’t quite real, but it’s fun to think about them as real.  Some people are very interested in leprechauns, and hunt for them in the woods.  Some people think that a good way to discover a leprechaun would be to find a pot of gold.  But there’s no evidence that leprechauns make pot of gold, or that they exist.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What do they have to do with rainbows?'}]
