DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Should you throw out your sponge or try to sanitize it?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Hm.  Well it sounds like you got confused by something you read about how sponges get germs, and were wondering if they need to be thrown away or can they be sanitized?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes which should I do?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'That’s a tricky question. In general, when you read something online, it’s better to seek a second opinion. But in this case, you can just tell me why you’re confused about this sponge and I’ll try to understand.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I use it do dishes. Does it get too dirty over time? Should I throw it out after so long?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Ok, that makes sense. But also, I notice you said “too dirty over time”, not “all the time”. So, it sounds like what you really need to know is when a sponge is really so disgusting that it’s not worth using?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes right.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Now you’re asking whether a sponge will be too dirty to use after a period of time? Let me help you out - you’ve probably heard that it’s good to have a few “clean” sponges on hand, right? And if you can reuse those sponges, it’s better than just throwing them out. So is it about getting them really “clean”, or is it more about their “usefulness”? If I’m wiping something up and get the whole sponge wet, is that enough to be unusable? Or if I’m wiping off a plate that’s already been cleaned off?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I don't want to spread germs. If they are beyond being useful I want to throw them out."}]
