DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is macular degeneration?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well, macular degeneration is a common condition where the cells in the center of the retina (called the macula) lose their ability to receive light.  This loss of visual function, usually due to damage to the blood vessels that supply the retina, leads to blindness in the affected eye(s).  The condition is very common in people over the age of 60.  It’s not treatable and cannot be reversed.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Do you know what damages the blood vessels that supply the retina?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, it’s caused by the build up of toxic deposits (called drusen) in the eye, similar to the build up of calcium deposits in your arteries that can lead to hardening of the arteries.  The leading causes of this build up of deposits are smoking, high cholesterol levels, high blood pressure, and diabetes.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can an ophthalmologist see the drusen?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, it’s possible for an ophthalmologist to see them with an ophthalmoscope, or a slit lamp.  They also can use a test called fluorescein angiography to look at the blood flow through the retina.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'And can the drusen be removed?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Not at the current time.  Even the fluorescein angiography can’t reliably show where the drusen are located.  The way this disease usually progresses is that first the blood flow slows down in the center of the retina.  In the early stages of the disease, a very low dose of a drug called a vasodilator (like Diamox) can improve the blood flow, but later this can’t be done.  The macular degeneration progresses and eventually the retina becomes disconnected from the central area of the brain that controls vision.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is testing being done to find a cure for macular degeneration?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, there’s a very large effort to find better methods of treating macular degeneration and to develop a cure, such as via gene therapy.  There’s also research to find the causes and improve treatments for the conditions that lead to the build up of drusen.  One major focus of this research is to understand how to prevent damage to the blood vessels that supply the retina in the first place.  This involves studying factors like oxidative stress, inflammation, and vascular damage.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can a healthy person develop macular degeneration?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, most people with macular degeneration have never smoked or had high cholesterol.  However, these conditions do increase your risk for macular degeneration.  And you can have macular degeneration without any of these risk factors.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What are early warning signs of macular degeneration?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The first symptoms are called “drusen” or yellow spots or flecks in the center of the eye.  These drusen usually appear in a person’s 50’s and are one of the signs of the condition.  As the disease progresses, the drusen increase in size and there is also “scarring” in the area of the retina that is fed by the retinal blood vessels.  Eventually the blood flow decreases and the person becomes blind in that eye.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Does someone slowly go blind when their macular degeneration advances or does it go quickly?'}]
