DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "My significant other recently moved across the country for work. He won't be back for at least another year. Do you have any advice on how to have a successful long-distance relationship?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'That’s an interesting question, and I’m not sure I’m the right person to ask. Can I ask why you’re asking?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I'm asking because I thought you might have some suggestions about keeping a relationship alive while me and my boyfriend are living apart from each other."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Oh, so I’m not totally wrong! Sorry for being a bit slow on that.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'So what do you suggest I do to keep our relationship vibrant and healthy while my boyfriend is away?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I don’t have any specific advice for you, since I haven’t experienced a long-distance relationship myself. In general though, communication is really important, and I’d suggest keeping in touch as often as possible. Are you planning on staying in the same place, or moving somewhere else?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I'm staying in the same place and waiting for him to return home. What do you suggest other than frequently keeping in touch?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think a lot of long-distance relationships end up getting into trouble when one person feels neglected and the other person is ignoring the needs of their partner, or focusing only on their own goals.  To try to avoid this, I’d suggest having frequent conversations and sharing as much as possible with each other.  That includes sharing your concerns and feelings and letting your partner know when they’re making you feel left out.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "That is helpful, thank you. Can you offer advice on what I should do when I'm really missing him or feeling lonely?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'When you’re missing your partner, that’s understandable.  I’d suggest spending some time alone with them when they’re in town, if possible.  Also, you might be able to get together with some friends in the same situation and see each other as often as you’re able to.  If that doesn’t work, you might also consider spending time with a friend who’s nearby, or making other social connections through work, or anything else.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I think I might try to connect with friends who are going through the same thing, that's great advice."}]
