DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "I'd like to look at new art. Are there any Art Deco artists you recommend?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Do you mean the period from around the 1920s through the 1940s, with forms influenced by the prewar avant-garde?  That was a time in which many European and American artists were going to Paris and other places in Europe to see modern art, and the interest in that led to these trends where artists designed objects and environments using more colorful styles, often with less focus on perspective and naturalism.  Some of the most famous examples are Tiffany lamps and the metal-mesh ceilings in the Woolworth Building in New York.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'That sounds right. Lots of curves and metal, like old style cars.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I could talk about how that style ended in the 1940s, after artists like Pablo Picasso, Jean Arp, and Piet Mondrian introduced more geometric styles, which eventually came to be called “Art Deco.”  Mondrian actually also drew many abstract geometric pictures in pencil, and that might be interesting for you.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Art Deco was earlier though, 1920s to 30s.'}]
