DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can worms reproduce without opposites sexes?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Worms are non-hermaphroditic, which means that they have separate sexes, and need to mate with another worm to reproduce.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I heard somewhere that they can be asexual, what does that mean?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Asexual reproduction just means that a worm doesn’t need a mate to reproduce. It can reproduce just by itself.  There are a few ways that a worm can reproduce asexually.  Worms can produce eggs without a male partner, and then when those eggs are ready to hatch they self-fertilize with their own sperm.  Or a worm can reproduce asexually by dividing into two worms, instead of producing eggs.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Divide into 2 worms?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, a worm can split into two worms without the presence of a male.  They can form “gemmules” on their body, which are basically chunks of the body that will grow up to become another worm.  The worm can then have a reproductive episode (in which it makes eggs) and then the two worms will grow separately from the one egg.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'That is strange.'}]
