DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "Hi, I have an invitation to go horseback riding with some friends next weekend. Can you explain the basics of riding a horse, so I don't get hurt or embarrass myself?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Sure, I'd love to.  Let's talk about how to sit on the horse, first of all.  There are many ways to do this, but there are a couple of main positions to consider.  The first one is to sit 'tall', which means sticking your bottom and back straight out, sitting up straight like this (keeping your shoulders high and relaxed) and using your thighs and lower legs to grip the horse.  The other one is 'low', which means keeping your bottom low, and using your knees to grip the horse, with your arms relaxed by your sides."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Okay. How do I get on the horse to begin with? And once I'm on it, how do I control it?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Okay, in both cases the basics are pretty similar.  First you'll need to find your horse and get to it.  It will be wearing a halter and you can slip the lead rope through it.  You'll then need to lead your horse by the rope to wherever you want to go.  To mount you will pull yourself up, with your right foot in the stirrup, and push your left leg as far forward as it will go.  Then you'll need to stand up by lifting up your leg and getting the rest of your body off the ground, keeping your balance by gripping the saddle with your right leg and the reins in your left hand.  To dismount you'll swing your left leg back and use your right leg to push yourself down to the ground, and then gently let go of the saddle and take your right foot out of the stirrup."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "All right. Once I'm on the horse, how do I make it stop, start, go left and right, or speed up and slow down?"}]
