DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is a gyro?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Gee-roh. It’s an ancient Greek unit of measure used to measure the rotation of an object. We still use this measure for astronauts on the moon and spacecraft in space.  A person at rest is a zero, and you add more for each rotation as they increase.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Okay! But I meant the food gyro.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'A gyro is the name for an assortment of foods like meat, onions, and peppers, served in a pita bread. It’s Greek food.  I hope that was helpful!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes, thanks. Can you give me a recipe for one?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Sure, here’s one for a chicken gyro:  Preheat oven to 425 degrees F. Slice chicken breast into thin strips and arrange on baking sheet. Season chicken with salt and pepper and brush with olive oil. Roast chicken in oven for 15 minutes. Flip chicken, drizzle with sauce and continue roasting for 10 minutes or until chicken reaches a minimum internal temperature of 160 degrees F. Top with feta, roasted red peppers, cucumbers, onions, black olives, and hummus. Enjoy!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Great! Do I serve it in a type of bread?'}]
