DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How did man discover fire?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not actually sure, but fire is generally a powerful source of heat, so people used it to cook and to get warmth.   In prehistoric times, this would have required a lot of labor, because a natural source of fire, like a volcano, would have been impractical to carry around with you.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I mean, do you think the first fire discovery was through a lightning-struck object, or perhaps a forest fire?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I don’t think we know for sure how fire was discovered, but I think many people would suggest a lightning strike.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Wouldn't the first human to have discovered fire be scared of it?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I don’t think they would.  Because fire is naturally occurring, I think the first humans to come across it would have no reason to fear it.  It would be new, but not threatening.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You have a point there. I guess they would worry more about the wild animals and bad weather.'}]
