DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you describe the agriculture done at Machu Picchu?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Machu Picchu was a small city, and as such needed to produce food to feed its residents. The area around Machu Picchu was primarily farmland, and given that Machu Picchu sits on a mountaintop, many plants were grown that thrive in high elevation climates.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How did they grow food on the mountain where they lived?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'They farmed what was called “terraced fields”. The area around Machu Picchu is very steep, and the best locations for growing crops was the areas that were less steep. Terraced fields were used to grow crops.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What did the terraced fields allow the Incas to accomplish?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The terraced fields allowed them to build lots of vertical space into their agriculture, allowing them to use the slope of the mountain.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How did it work when it rained?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Terraced fields were used when it rained, but the Incas also had other methods of growing food when it was not raining.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What I mean is, how did the rain affect the terrace fields'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Rain was a concern for the people who grew food at Machu Picchu. Some crops would need more water than others. For instance, lettuce and cilantro are the kinds of crops that would need a lot of water. This was dealt with by building a terrace for every crop that required more water, for instance, in a single terraced field, there would be lettuce terraces, followed by the cilantro terraces.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Thanks for your help'}]
