DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the Noble Peace Price?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The Nobel Peace Prize is a prestigious award that recognizes individuals, organizations, or nations that contribute toward the most important achievements in the field of peace.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Who invented it?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The idea behind the prize is credited to Alfred Nobel’s will, which stipulated that his fortune was to be awarded to those who made the “greatest contribution to the promotion of peace.” The first Nobel Peace Prize was awarded in 1901, the first recipient being Henry Dunant.  Since 1901, the peace prize has been awarded to 132 individuals and 20 organizations.  The prizes are awarded in a ceremony held in Oslo every year.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Is Alfred Nobel's trust still funding the prizes?  Where do they get the money now?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Alfred Nobel’s last will and testament, which has the best claim to legitimacy, stipulated that the monetary gift be administered through the Nobel Foundation, which has the mandate to invest the assets to ensure their viability for future generations. The foundation today is one of the world’s largest wealth funds, with assets of over $1.4 billion.  It uses its resources to administer, protect, and invest the assets awarded in Nobel prizes, and make grants in selected areas. The foundation's latest allocation, for fiscal year 2016, totaled more than $6 billion."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Other than Alfred Nobel, did any other organizations or people contribute ?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'During the 20th century, the fund was replenished by the Swedish central bank.  From 2013 onward, the fund has been supported by the Norwegian government.  The Norwegian Ministry of Finance is the body that approves and implements the awards, while the Norwegian Nobel Committee selects the prize winners.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is the original fund used up?'}]
