DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How does a computer work'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Great question! Well, there’s a lot of complexity, but I can try to explain what’s going on. A computer’s CPU is one of the most complex pieces of hardware around.  Basically a CPU consists of lots of “logic gates” which are like simple electrical circuits, except instead of electricity, they handle ones and zeroes.  A lot of the decisions a computer makes are “asynchronous,” which is a fancy way of saying they just happen without asking anyone for permission.  The central processor and its memory communicate over an electrical bus called “busses” which allow many processors and memory to work together.  When the central processor sends a request to another processor, or memory, it usually sends along the “address” of the thing it wants, so that the recipient can find the location.  These addresses are also in memory, and they are usually stored on a so-called “memory address bus” which is physically different from the central processor’s bus.  The memory access time is much slower than the central processor’s bus, and so in most modern systems, the central processor has a “cache” that is separate from the main memory.  It is designed to be faster and closer in proximity to the central processor.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'can you explain it a little more easier'}]
