DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Could you explain to me how sunscreen protects your skin from the sun?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I can do my best! You probably know that the sun produces ultraviolet radiation (UV-R). This is invisible to the naked eye and comes in three forms, called UVA, UVB and UVC. UVA is the type that causes skin damage over time, and therefore sunscreen needs to protect from UVA. The active ingredients of sunscreens are UV absorbers, which absorb UV-R in the UVB and UVC range.  It’s important that sunscreens are safe and effective, and protect against both UVA and UVB and UVC, so that you are protected from sunlight while wearing it.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the name of these UV absorbers?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Some common ingredients are oxybenzone and octinoxate.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Are these chemicals safe for long term use on your skin?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Sunscreen is generally considered safe.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What are the dangers of not using Sunscreen?'}]
