DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What exactly is dyslexia?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well, that’s a difficult question, and a complex one. Can you tell me more?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I'm interesting in learning about dyslexia."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "OK, great, that's one thing I can definitely help with."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Great. Is dyslexia a malfunction in the brain or something else?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "It's certainly the brain, but dyslexia is a medical condition that affects a small but important part of our language-processing system.  It's also important to understand that dyslexia is just one kind of problem.  There are many kinds of reading issues, like dyslexia, dysgraphia, and dyspraxia, to name a few, and all have different causes."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "How does the brain's language-processing system work?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "The brain does a lot of things, but we'll narrow it down to the language-processing system.  All of the mental computations that help us to understand written words and sentences take place in the brain's Broca's area, which is located in the frontal lobes.  There are also different types of Broca's area (bilateral, left-lateralized, and right-lateralized) that are involved with particular types of speech and language processing.  For dyslexia, Broca's area fails to connect with other parts of the brain, leading to visual problems (sometimes) and problems with reading."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Okay. So how does a speech therapist work with someone to correct a problem like dyslexia?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Dyslexia is most commonly treated by a speech therapist with a combination of strategies that I'm happy to discuss with you."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Sure, I'd like to hear more about the strategies a speech therapist uses."}]
