DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Do you know why humans cannot digest corn?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Corn, or maize, is a member of the grass family. Humans can digest the protein in corn, but they cannot digest the fiber in the kernels. There are more than one reason that humans cannot digest corn. For instance, the human digestive system lacks the enzyme that is needed to break down the protein in the corn kernel. Also, the pH level of the human stomach is too low to digest the fiber. Additionally, the corn kernel is toxic to the small intestine of a human. Corn has a concentration of cyanogenic glycosides which can be broken down by the stomach of an animal, but not by a human.  I should also mention that “humans” includes chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas, and humans.  A number of these animals can digest the protein in the corn, but it appears that the corn kernels are too toxic to digest in the small intestine of these animals as well.  Other plant matter can be processed by the small intestine, though it must first be broken down in the stomach.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'That is interesting'}]
