DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What are some documents I should get prepared if I want a divorce?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'We might want to collect some divorce documents, including a signed divorce agreement, a written separation agreement, a list of assets, and a list of debts. We should also collect evidence of parenting time and parenting plan arrangements, as well as custody agreements.  There’s also the “Honeymoon” effect and couples counseling, if you think it’d be helpful.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What if they are fighting against the custody agreement?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You’ll also want to be sure to document the fact that the other party has been invited to parenting time, but hasn’t responded.  If the other parent has an history of violence, they might need to be informed that legal action will be taken if they don’t start following the parenting plan.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the best way to communicate with that parent to be used as evidence in the courts?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How can I make things easier on my children during a divorce?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You might want to offer them some simple, age-appropriate explanations about what divorce means and why their family is changing. You might also think about how your divorce impacts them, and whether it’s better to make decisions in advance to try to reduce the disruptions to their day-to-day lives.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is there anything I should try to avoid doing?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'As the primary caregiver, it’s best to make the changes to your child’s routine that are needed in order to get through this transition in a timely way. Don’t make any sudden changes that could upset your children. They might be worried about a new childcare situation or not know where to go after school, and so they’re extra upset when changes are happening.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What should I do if I notice them acting different than usual?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'As the primary caregiver, it’s a good idea to have frequent conversations with your children to check on how they are feeling. Take the time to listen to them and respond to their feelings. Try to be as honest as you can with them. Kids often understand things better when they hear the truth, even when the truth is hard to hear.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Thank you.'}]
