DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How many votes are needed in the Electoral College to become President?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Fifty percent plus one.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How many electors cast votes in the US Electoral College?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '538.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How is that number determined?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'All states have a number of votes equal to the number of Senators and Representatives that they have.  States that have a fractional number of votes each get rounded up.   All states also have at least three votes.   The state of Nebraska, which has two votes in the US Senate, and is divided into two equally populated electoral districts, has three votes in the US Electoral College.  That’s because Nebraska is the only state with exactly three seats in the Senate.   The District of Columbia, a federal district, which has no Senate seats, gets three votes in the US Electoral College.  Its statehood is confirmed by the US Congress.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Which state has the most votes?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'California has 55, California is the state with the most electoral votes.   The next most electoral votes are Texas with 38, then New York with 29, then Illinois with 20, then Pennsylvania with 20.   Washington D.C. has 3 votes.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Goodbye'}]
