DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I marry someone who lives in another country?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'There are legal ways to marry someone in another country, and we could look up the law in your country to see how it works. But a common solution is to live in the same country as the person you want to marry, as then you can have the same legal rights as a married couple and do everything together.  So you’d need to move somewhere to be together with your partner, and live there in a way that you think is a good compromise.  We can look up statistics to see how many people live with their spouse and whether you’d have enough support.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I live in the u.s. and he lives in Spain.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'If you live in the United States and your partner lives in Spain, then a traditional solution is that you move to Spain.  The idea would be to see if this is a long-term solution that is good for both of you.  If moving is not an option, there are legal alternatives like registering as a civil union or common law marriage.  This would give you certain rights as a couple, but it is not a legal marriage in the US and it’s not clear if you could transfer the rights to another country or get the same social support in other countries.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Will I be able to return to the U.S.?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Once you’re married, you’re married.  It’s unlikely that you’ll be able to travel back to the United States after being married in Spain.  But your partner might also find it difficult to travel back to the United States, since they won’t be a legal resident or citizen of the United States.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How can they legally come to the states?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What are some fun crafts for a toddler?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Hm, crafts with toddlers seem quite tricky!  I recommend they get started young on things they can make on their own, like building towers out of stacking cups and/or drinking straws, or building towers out of foam letters and letters.  And there’s also something quite magical about gluing noodles onto play-dough to make shapes, which is a fun skill to get little ones started on.  Around age three or four, they should start doing more advanced projects, like using ribbons to make paper chains and paper windmills, and cutting up construction paper to make collages.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Okay, what else can you tell me?'}]
