DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "It was snowing the other day and I saw lightning. How can there be lightning and thunder when it's snowing?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The normal weather cycle doesn’t work exactly the same in the Arctic region as it does here in the south, and the difference is especially strong during the winter.  Normally, low-pressure systems rotate around the polar region from west to east.  But during the winter, when the air has been losing heat to the water for weeks or months, the cold air comes into contact with the much colder air below it.  This produces extreme levels of instability, causing intense updrafts that can reach far into the stratosphere.  In these conditions, thunderstorms can form at altitudes of over three kilometers!  The pressure at these altitudes is a factor of three less than at sea level, so strong updrafts often cause the air to rise very quickly, which can cause lightning.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'In what states or areas of the United States is lightning in winter most common?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'In the middle of the country, the Rocky Mountains, the Great Plains, and the High Plains states all have some of the most dangerous conditions for lightning.  The highest concentration of thunderstorms is in the south, near the Gulf Coast.  Thunderstorms also occur to some degree in every state.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Remember I\'m talking about winter thunderstorms, or "thundersnow". You\'re answering about summertime thunderstorms.'}]
