DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "I've read that its possible, but very rare, that someone remains conscious during surgery even though they got the anesthesia. Is this true?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The likelihood of this is so small that the FDA classifies it as "rare".  From 1 out of approximately 1 million surgeries, 1 in 50,000 will be incompletely anesthetized.  However, you should realize that this does not mean that they are in pain.  From many studies, they have found that if a person is going to experience this kind of pain, they have to be actually awake.  This means that they must be conscious while they are still in a position to feel pain, but also the anesthesia must not have been enough to make them fully unconscious.  Furthermore, it doesn\'t really make sense to ask whether it\'s possible, since this has never been measured in a way that is a useful benchmark for making decisions about treatment.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "That is pretty terrifying to think about. If it happens to someone, is it the doctor's fault?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "If someone is fully anesthetized, they are not supposed to be conscious during the operation.  However, sometimes things happen that prevent them from being fully anesthetized.  So, in some cases the doctor's actions may make things worse for the patient.  In some cases, there is no clear reason why the doctor's actions made a difference.  But in other cases, they were negligent in some way.  There are many ways that the doctor's actions may not be enough to cause the patient to wake up, though.  In cases where this happens, it's the hospital's responsibility to make sure that the patient has been sufficiently anesthetized.  The surgeon is usually not responsible, since they are not the ones who give the anesthetic.  However, they do have the responsibility to tell the anesthetist what they are doing and so that the anesthetist knows what they will do in the case that the patient wakes up and is able to feel pain.  This is because the doctor is considered the one in charge of the operation, and so they are responsible for making sure the patient is adequately anesthetized."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I see. Well thankfully this almost never happens.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, it\'s very rare.  Anesthetists even prepare themselves to not be responsible for this situation.  In case it ever happens to them, they are taught to say "It\'s not my fault" if anyone asks them.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "That sounds like they wouldn't be taking responsibility."}]
