DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'The planet is more water than land. I wonder where all the water came from?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not sure, but I think it could have come from ice coming down from the sky, or from Earth coming together from particles of ice or rock.  I’m not totally sure on the details.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Ice from the sky? Like comets?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes!  There’s evidence that comets from the outer Solar System, which aren’t the same thing as the Solar System itself, have brought water and other things to Earth.   There’s also evidence that ice might have come from inside Earth, from ice in the ground, but I don’t know how it got there.   My guess would be that maybe there was liquid water at the core of the planet, and over time, some of that has been released by the core and come up to the surface.   So it might have been under the surface for a long time, and the heat of the core caused the water to evaporate, and come up to the surface.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I have heard about the idea that water was trapped in the rocks that formed early Earth.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes!  It’s called “the primitive ocean hypothesis”, and it’s a reasonable guess.  For example, it’s possible that lava from volcanoes could have carried minerals and water up from the Earth’s interior.   There’s a theory that the first oceans were formed this way, by the volcanoes of the early Earth.   However, some scientists doubt that there was enough water in the early Earth to produce oceans.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'The moon has water?'}]
