DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I track steps with an Apple watch?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think you need to download the Walk app and go to the menu.  Then, select “Add Goal”.  It should help you track steps in the future, without your phone present.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I didn't know it was that easy.  Can you set a step goal?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "That doesn't seem to be available.  I guess it might change in future updates."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Oh, I guess I should get a step calculator then.  Thanks.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You’re welcome.  I just started using one myself, and I think I can suggest something similar, that may suit your purpose better: https://www.google.com/search?q=digital+steps+counter+calculator'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Thanks!'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I decrease my power bill?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well, I think you need to consider your options. On average, people in America pay about $1,100 a month for electricity, but in some places it can go as high as $1,600. You’ll want to consider how much energy you’re using at home, and how it might be saved. I’ll explain a few different options.  \nFirst, you could use a programmable thermostat, and set it to turn off your air conditioner and heat when it’s not needed.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I have my thermostat on auto so it shuts off when it reaches my desired temperature. What else can I do to save on electricity?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Second, you could switch to LED lighting, or install some motion sensor light bulbs so that the lights don’t come on when nobody’s around.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I live in a studio and I am always home, so I don’t think that will help. Do you have any other suggestions?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Third, there’s usually an energy efficient lightbulb option in stores that will have you spending less. Or if you don’t want to buy new bulbs, you could try changing the bulbs in your lights to energy efficient ones.  Are you paying more than you should for your electricity?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes. I have been paying $180 for 580 square foot studio, which is way too much.'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I want to sew a fitted sheet for my queen sized mattress and want to know how much fabric to buy.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'OK, so sewing fitted sheets can be a bit tricky because of how the threads twist on the bed if they’re sewn tightly, but that can be done, you just need to make sure to have a minimum of 1/4" of seam allowance around the edges of the mattress. That’s because the mattress flexes on all sides, and so it can move the fabric from its intended position if it’s sewn too tightly. That said, you’ll want to decide what your final size will be after taking the mattress size into account, because if you don’t, you may end up wasting a lot of fabric. What’s your final size going to be?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Well, the fabric I want to use comes in spools that are 5 feet wide and my queen-sized mattress is 18" deep.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Right, so that means you’ll need a piece of fabric 16 feet by 4 feet to sew the fitted sheet. That’s roughly 54 square feet. How much are you planning to spend?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I've already chosen the fabric, so I will spend whatever I need to spend on that fabric. The fabric is 5 feet wide, so what length will I need?"}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How hot is the surface of the Sun?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well, it’s really hard to know for sure. The number is different depending on which part of the Sun we’re measuring. The sunspot-facing side of the Sun is about 5,800 degrees Fahrenheit (3,030 degrees Celsius). The back side of the Sun is about 1,000 degrees Fahrenheit (540 degrees Celsius) cooler.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How does the Sun create so much energy?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The Sun is a star, which means it’s really, really hot, and the energy comes from nuclear fusion reactions.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What makes something a Star?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'In astronomy, a star is a massive, luminous ball of hot plasma in space. A star is a huge nuclear fusion reactor made of gases, which produces a huge amount of energy.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Cool.'}]
