DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What do I teach kids about stranger danger?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think most kids around the age of ten to twelve have a basic understanding of what “stranger danger” is, and if you’ve talked to them about it before, then it may be something that they’ll remember and know what to do.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I want to teach a younger kid'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Hmm, I think the way you would do that with a younger child is the same way you would do it with an older child, which is just talking about being safe in general and then making it very concrete.  So you might start by drawing a map of the area where you live.  It might help to draw an x to show where you live, and then draw lines that show where you sometimes go.  You could draw houses that are your friends’ houses and maybe add circles to show where kids’ homes are, or playgrounds, or maybe some of your favorite places.  You could also make a little marker for your house, and draw in circles around other houses or places you like to go.  Then, you could mark some places that you’re not allowed to go, which would be the neighbor’s house, any dangerous places, or places where your friends live but you’re not allowed to go there unless you’re there with your friends.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'okay, what can i tell them about approaching strangers, or strangers approaching them?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'If you’re talking about a child approaching a stranger, I think you can talk about that by being very open with them.  You can let them know that sometimes it’s good to go up to a stranger and talk to them, but other times it might be better to not approach them, like if they’re in a store or at the beach.  If you’re talking about strangers approaching them, I think you can give them some concrete examples like “Sometimes adults on the street might ask you if you want to play, or they might want to tell you how cute you are.”'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I don't think it will ever be okay for them to approach strangers."}]
