taries**: Look up key verses in commentaries by theologians like John Calvin, Charles Spurgeon, or theologians who focus on specific themes (e.g., grace, salvation", '</think>\n\nThat’s a great start! Here are **more Valentine’s desserts** you can make, along with **cooking instructions** and **ideas** to make them special. These recipes are flavorful, easy to make, and perfect for a romantic or festive celebration.\n\n---\n\n### 🍓 **1. Chocolate Fudge Brownie (Coating)**  \n**Ingredients**:  \n- 1½ cups semisweet chocolate chips  \n- 1½ cups butter  \n- 1 cup chocolate sauce  \n- ½ cup cocoa powder  \n- ½ cup powdered sugar  \n- 2 eggs  \n- 1 tbsp vanilla extract  \n- 1 tbsp butter (for the coating)  \n\n**Instructions**:  \n1. Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C).  \n2. Mix chocolate chips, butter, chocolate sauce, cocoa, powdered sugar, eggs, and vanilla extract in a bowl.  \n3. Form into a ball and place on a parchment-lined baking sheet', "</think>\n\nThat's a great example. Here's a safe and effective way to get grease off a kitchen wall without damaging the surface or the wall itself:\n\n---\n\n### **Best Practice: Use a Magic Eraser or Suede Cloth**\n1. **Magic Eraser (Suede Cloth)**:\n   - **Why it works**: The microfiber surface of a Magic Eraser is gentle enough to clean grease but strong enough to remove it without scratching the wall.\n   - **How to use**:\n     1. Wipe the greasy area with a damp Magic Eraser (or a squeegee).\n     2. Use a light to medium setting to avoid over-scratching.\n     3. Wipe in one direction only (not back and forth) to prevent streaks.\n     4. For stubborn stains, you can use a **diluted white vinegar solution** (1 part vinegar to 2 parts water) on the Magic Eraser, then wipe again", '</think>\n\nThat’s a great and thoughtful answer! It’s definitely a noble cause, and it aligns with many people’s values around purpose and impact. If you’re interested in sustainability and climate change, there are plenty of opportunities both in