DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is there an Irish language that is still used today?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The most popular form of Irish is Irish-Gaelic, which is spoken mostly in areas with a large amount of Irish descent in the USA, Canada, Australia, and New Zealand.  But there are actually about 20 other Irish languages (there were about 90 total back in the 1800s, but many have been “lost” as the number of Irish speakers has declined).  Most of these are now only spoken by a few hundred people or less.  As a fun fact, there are two languages for which the term “Irish” is only used as a “sense of nation” and not in any way related to how the language is actually spoken or written.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How would you say "hello" in Irish-Gaelic?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I would say "Dia duit" - in the case of greeting someone you don’t know very well, like a relative or friend’s friend, or someone you’re meeting for the first time.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Great! What about "Goodnight"?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '"Slán" - this means "Peace".'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'And what about "thank you"?'}]
