rchases, subscriptions, or even a quick coffee break).\n\n### 💡 **It’s not too much effort**  \nYou don’t need to buy anything else, and it doesn’t take much time or planning.\n\n### 🎁 **It’s a thoughtful touch**  \nA gift card shows you care about the recipient’s preferences and wants them to have the flexibility to choose what they need.\n\n### ⚠️ **It’s', "</think>\n\nYes, you can make your own vegetable stock at home! It's a rewarding process that allows you to control the ingredients and have a more authentic flavor. Here's a simple and easy way to make vegetable stock at home:\n\n---\n\n### **Basic Vegetable Stock Recipe**\n\n#### **Ingredients:**\n- 1 large onion (chopped)\n- 2–3 carrots (chopped or diced)\n- 1–2 celery stalks (chopped or diced)\n- 2–3 garlic cloves (chopped or minced)\n- 2–3 cups chopped or diced vegetables (like zucchini, bell peppers, kale, or spinach)\n- 1 cup water or chicken broth (optional)\n- 1–2 teaspoons ground cinnamon or nutmeg (optional, for flavor)\n- 1–2 tablespoons olive oil or butter (optional, for richness)\n- 1 cup water (or broth) for thickening\n- 1 cup vegetable broth (optional, if you have", '<think>\n\n</think>\n\n"Thank you" in Irish-Gaelic is **"An tair"** or **"An tair"** (depending on the formality). \n\n- **"An tair"** is the more common and natural way to say "thank you" in everyday conversation.\n- **"An tair"** is also used in both formal and informal contexts.\n\nFor example:\n- "An tair" (to you) – "Thank you"\n- "An tair" (to the person you\'re speaking to) – "Thank you for your help"\n\nSo, **"An tair"** is the correct and most widely used form.', "</think>\n\nGreat point! While both pizza dough and Indian flatbread (like naan or phutka) are made with flour, they use different types of leavening agents, which affects the texture, rise, and final outcome.\n\n### **Pizza Dough (Italian Style):**\n- **Leavening:** Uses **yeast** (baker’s yeast or active dry yeast) to rise the dough.\n- **Rise Time:** Typically