DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Which is better, and ipad or a kindle fire tablet?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'This depends a lot on what you’re trying to do with your tablet.  The iPad has much more storage space and more powerful processors.  A tablet can be a great second screen for a laptop, for example, but the Kindle Fire tablet has many other features that may make it easier for you to use.  What do you want to do with the device?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I want to read email and watch netflix'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'To do that, the Kindle Fire is probably a better choice, because you’re not going to need much storage space or a lot of speed.  As far as I’m aware, there are no full-blown email apps for the iPad, which is why it’s not a good choice for email.  Watching Netflix on an iPad is pretty much the same as watching Netflix on the Kindle Fire.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How are they for web surfing?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The Kindle Fire is actually not very good at this.  It has decent support for the open-source web browser called "FireFox" but not great support for Google\'s Chrome, Safari, or Opera.  That’s because of the way that the Kindle Fire tablet is built.  It’s built from a modified version of the Android operating system that’s designed to be very good at running small programs for smartphones.   For web browsing, there’s a “mini browser” called Amazon Silk that also supports streaming audio and video, but it’s slow.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is there any way to put Chrome on the kindle fire?'}]
