DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some South African  dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Durban Bunny Chow?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'A “bunny chow” is a type of African curry, and it is usually served as a gravy over rice, with meat as the main ingredient. Some versions have chunks of carrots or squash in it, and some have lots of fresh ginger and curry leaves. In a traditional recipe, the meat is taken off the bone and cooked down with onions and spices, then reheated with stock or water and perhaps a little tomato puree. If you want to try a similar dish that doesn’t have meat, I would recommend just cutting up a sweet potato, and then cutting up one or two large onions, and cooking that together in some water or stock until it’s tender, then adding some salt and pepper. I hope you enjoy the meal!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Isn't this supposed to be served in a hallowed out piece of bread?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "I don't know what you mean by that, but I'll try to look it up! Do you want me to tell you about any other South African dishes or drinks?"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'No, this is what I found. A bunny-chow is basically made from half a loaf of bread (with the inside scooped out and a kept to dip in the gravy). The hollow loaf is then filled with delicious authentic curry- either made from lamb, mutton or vegetables.  Beef, chicken or mince can also be used.'}]
