DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you explain a bit about how Chinese characters mean different things?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Sure. What do you mean?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I mean, one chinese character can mean a whole English sentence. Is that right?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The exact meaning can be a little complicated to explain.  But one character usually has several different meanings that are sometimes different to Western languages. For example, 舔 can be:'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I don't believe that at all, those things are way too different"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Most of these are words that aren’t often used by English speakers, but they might be quite common in Chinese.  Even English words can mean several different things, although usually they’ll only have one meaning in a particular context.  The word chair is an example of that.  In English you can say: "a chair is sitting", “the chair was made by Alain", "she sat on the chair", "I pulled the chair over", “the chair is empty”, “I sat in a chair”, “we need some more chairs”, “we are dealing with furniture”, “the electrician was fixing a chair”, “I was sitting in a chair”, “they fixed the chair”, “she sat in a chair”.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'All of those words are often used by English speakers. You are making things up and not making sense.'}]
