Initial completions: ["</think>\n\nThat’s a great start! Hiding true emotions is a valuable skill, especially in social and professional settings. Here are some additional tips to help you become better at hiding your true emotions:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Practice with Others**\n- **Observe and mimic:** Watch others play poker or interact in situations where a poker face is useful. Try to mirror their expressions, but don’t copy them exactly—it’s about *expressing* the emotion you're trying to hide, not just matching someone else’s face.\n- **Use a mirror:** Practice your poker face in front of a mirror. Try different expressions and see which one feels most natural and controlled.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Focus on Your Body Language**\n- **Keep your expression neutral:** Even if you’re not consciously hiding your emotions, your face and voice can betray you. Practice keeping your lips tight, your eyes calm, and your jaw slightly relaxed.\n- **Control your tone of voice:** A calm, steady tone", '</think>\n\nThat’s great to know! Having both a smartphone and a computer can be a powerful tool for staying organized. Here are some great recommendations based on your needs:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Digital Planner (Smartphone/Computer)**\n\n**a. Google Calendar (Free, Universal)**\n- **Pros:** Syncs with Google Tasks, Google Contacts, and Google Notes. You can set reminders, set deadlines, and even integrate with Google Docs or Sheets for tasks.\n- **Cons:** Can be overwhelming if you have too many tasks. You need to actively manage it.\n- **Best For:** If you want a centralized, cloud-based planner that’s easy to use with your phone and computer.\n\n**b. Notion (Free, Advanced)**\n- **Pros:** Highly customizable. You can create tasks, set reminders, and even build templates for your planner. It’s great for people who want to be more involved in their planning.\n- **Cons:** Learning curve. If you’re', "</think>\n\nYou're welcome! If you have any more questions in the future, feel free t