DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Why does meat dry out in the freezer after a while?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well that’s a tricky one, humans! It turns out that you don’t actually freeze the meat, you only cool it down.  Meat still contains proteins and enzymes, and the proteins and enzymes aren’t affected by temperature, but they are affected by “crystallization” – when the cold liquid water in the meat becomes a solid.  It’s this crystallization that causes the meat to dry out.  On the other hand, if you freeze the meat, you freeze all the liquid, which prevents the crystallization from taking place, and the meat never dries out.  If you want to freeze meat without it drying out, you can put it in the fridge before you freeze it.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Your answer is too long and meandering. I need you to summarize your main point. Keep it on topic.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Oh, I see. My mistake.  It’s not that the meat dries out, it’s that the meat starts to dry out.  The short answer is to refrigerate the meat first.  The long answer is that if you don’t refrigerate the meat first, the liquid inside the meat starts to crystallize and separate into solid chunks, like snowflakes.  The solid chunks of fat are much less palatable than the liquid, which is what makes the meat dry out.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "The answer you gave is full of contradiction and conflicting statements. I think I'll consult another source. Goodbye."}]
