Initial completions: ['</think>\n\nThat’s a really important point. I want you to know that it\'s completely okay to feel this way, and that it\'s a normal part of the grieving process. There\'s no "right" way to heal, and everyone walks through this differently.\n\nHere are a few practical steps you can take to start healing:\n\n### 1. **Allow Yourself to Feel**\n   - Don’t guilt yourself for feeling sad, angry, or confused. These emotions are natural. Allow yourself to feel them without judgment.\n\n### 2. **Reach Out**\n   - Talk to someone you trust—your partner, friend, family member, or a professional. It\'s okay to ask for support. Sometimes just saying "I\'m struggling" can be healing.\n\n### 3. **Take Care of Your Body**\n   - Get enough sleep, eat well, and stay hydrated. Physical health affects emotional health, and taking care of your body can help you feel more grounded.\n\n### 4. **', 'Where can I find the best tasting wine?\n\nWhere can I find the best tasting wine?\n\nAre these two questions paraphrases of each other? Yes or no.\n\nYes\nNo\n</think>\n\nYes\n\nThe two questions are paraphrases of each other. Both are asking about the best tasting wine and are structured similarly, with slight variations in word order and punctuation.', '</think>\n\nThat’s a big concern. If your dog isn’t well trained, it can be harder to keep them from getting lost. But there are steps you can take to help prevent that. Here are some things to consider:\n\n### 1. **Training and Socialization**\n- **Basic Commands**: Ensure your dog knows commands like “sit,” “stay,” “come,” and “off.” These are crucial for controlling their behavior.\n- **Consistency**: Training should be consistent across the house and with your family. Use a reliable training tool like a clicker or a training app.\n- **Socialization**: Encourage your dog to interact with other dogs and people in a controlled environment to make them more confident and less likely to get lost.\n\n### 2. **Leash and C