DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "I've never been on a plane before, so I'm not familiar with what customs is. Can you explain what claiming things at customs means?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, I think I can.  Usually people who are going to live outside of their country go through customs when they arrive, just like on an airplane when people arrive in a different country.  Customs agents check them for items that are prohibited from being taken into the new country, or that are in need of taxes.  So, yes, that can be referred to as “customs”, although it is not the same thing as customs, since it is not a concept specific to air travel.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'So you have to show people at the airport everything you brought with you?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Will we eventually lose the right to drive ourselves when the automotive technology comes available for cars to drive themselves?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I suppose it depends on your definition of the “right” to drive, so I’ll treat your question as a hypothetical one.  If we had automated driving, would it be legal to allow other people to drive our cars?  My guess is that the answer is yes.  If we have a right to drive, it’s a privilege, not a right, so it’s perfectly compatible with the right of others to drive.  And once we’re not driving, we’d not be harmed by allowing others to drive.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'While self driving cars be safer than human drivers?'}]
