ices (EMS) personnel**  \n\nTheir work is essential in saving lives and reducing', "</think>\n\nYes, the Pashto language is no longer spoken in Pakistan. Here's a bit more detailed information to help you understand this:\n\n- **Historical Context**: Pashto was spoken across a vast region in South Asia, from the Indus Valley to the Indian subcontinent. It was a major language of the Pashtun people, who are one of the largest ethnic groups in Afghanistan.\n\n- **Decline in Pakistan**: The Pashto language was a significant part of the cultural and linguistic identity of the Pashtun people in Pakistan. However, after the 1947 Partition of British India, the Pashtun community was split. The Pashtuns in the north were integrated into the newly formed Pakistan, while those in the south and east were absorbed into the Indian subcontinent.\n\n- **Current Status**: While Pashto is still spoken in Afghanistan, it is not the official language of Pakistan. The official languages of Pakistan are Urdu and English. However"]
