DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the process for trial of a new drug?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'This depends on the context. There are multiple standards and methods for testing of medicines and drugs, such as clinical trials, animal testing, lab studies, field studies, etc. The specifics of the testing are different depending on the type of medication, the stage of the drug’s development, the expected effects of the drug, etc. Generally a new drug can be tested in several ways: by giving it to patients in a clinical trial (test/proof of concept), by studying it in animal studies (1st phase) to determine the safety and the dose, by testing it on healthy people in a placebo-controlled study (2nd phase) to verify the safety and the effects, and by using “field studies” (3rd phase) to evaluate it in real-world conditions, e.g. on a larger population, in different cultural settings, etc. Once a drug has been shown to be safe and effective in animal studies and in clinical trials, it is approved for use, and then it can be manufactured and sold.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "What's the timeline for a drug going to clinical trial all the way to when it's released for purchase?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The process for approval varies by drug and the country, but I can give some examples. For example, the approval timeline for the drug “Epclusa” for the treatment of blood clots in patients with a certain type of cancer took 7 years. And the approval timeline for the drug “Lescol” for treatment of high cholesterol was 5 years.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Interesting. Why do some drugs have a generic alternative?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The reason for the availability of generic versions of drugs is that the patents on the original drug expire after a certain number of years, allowing for the creation of generic drugs with the same pharmacological profile. So although there is always a slight difference in the formulation of the two drugs (e.g. the color, taste, shape), the ingredients are the same, and the difference between the two is mainly due to its label.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'It seems like a lot of drugs have a scary list of side effects. Are they just being overly cautious or are these serious effects that should cause concern in people who take them?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'There are many reasons for the long list of side effects. First of all, the more of an effect a drug has, the more likely it is that it will also have unintended side effects. Another reason is that since the diseases it is treating are very serious, the side effects are also taken very seriously. The drug companies are also required to report any negative results they find during the testing and development process, such as anaphylaxis or other unexpected side effects, even if the effect is seen in a very small number of people (1 in 10,000, for example). And finally, some drugs can have the same “side effect” in different people, e.g. in 1 person the drug may cause drowsiness, while in another it may cause diarrhea. Also, some side effects are not severe but may still be unwanted, e.g. in some people a drug may cause weight gain.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'And what kinds of medication have a risk of dependence?'}]
