DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What was Ancient Rome like?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'When the Roman Empire was at its height, it controlled a large area in the Mediterranean Sea.  It’s thought that in this time, about 50 million people lived in the Empire, and that Rome had at least half a million residents.  Rome became an important center for commerce, politics, art, literature, and religion.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What else can you tell me about it?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'During its height, Rome and its empire was built on the conflict between the patricians and the plebeians.  It was a conflict between two groups of the same race, the Romans, although from opposite ends of the social spectrum.  The plebeians were the majority of the population, but were living as serfs, or the lowest class of free citizen.  The patricians were the elite group of society, those who controlled Rome politically and economically.  There were about four million plebeians, and about thirty thousand patricians, and they lived very differently.  One way this conflict manifested itself was in the different roles that the two groups had in the government of the empire.  Plebeians could not hold the magistracies, which were the highest offices in government.  There was also tension between the patricians and the plebeians because the rich patricians were greedy for power and land, whereas the plebeians wanted more rights.  This conflict led to a number of rebellions.  It was possible for the conflict to be resolved peacefully, as it did at one point, but this only lasted for a few years before it erupted again.  Eventually the plebeians'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Thanks'}]
