DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "I think I might need my wisdom teeth removed, but I'm nervous about the surgery. Do dentists use localized or general anesthesia for wisdom teeth removal?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'That’s a great question. It’s hard to predict exactly how your body will react, but it usually doesn’t require general anesthesia. General anesthesia puts you to sleep, and this may be necessary for more complex surgeries, but in wisdom teeth removal, it’s usually safer and more effective to use local anesthesia, which numbs a small area.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I had a friend who got his wisdom teeth removed as an adult, and they gave him general anesthesia.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'This is rare, but sometimes people’s bodies don’t react well to local anesthesia. It might help you to discuss this with your dentist, because the right anesthesia option really depends on your individual circumstances.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "The dentist said I'd likely be getting general anesthesia, since I'm an adult and he needs ot remove four wisdom teeth."}]
