The water is at the **highest temperature** and **moves in a continuous, rhythmic motion**, like waves on the surface.\n\n### 2. **Why does the water "roll over"?**\nWhen water is boiling, it\'s being heated by the heat source. As the temperature of the water rises, the **boiling point** increases. But at the **surface**, the water is **heated by the air** (from the heat source) and **cools down by evaporation** (as water is turned into vapor). This process creates a **cycle** of energy between heat, mass, and energy.\n\n### 3. **The "surface area" of the water:**\nWhen the water is boiling vigorously', '</think>\n\nThe **Great Pyramid of Giza** is located in the Egyptian desert, in the Egyptian state of **Faiyum**, near the Nile River. It is situated in the **Giza Necropolis**, which is a large necropolis in the **Faiyum region** of Egypt. The pyramid is located on a large flat area of the desert, and it is part of a larger complex that includes other ancient structures, such as the **Pyramid of Khufu** (also known as the Great Pyramid of Cheops) and the **Pyramid of Khafre**.\n\nThe Great Pyramid is one of the **Seven Wonders of the World** and is considered one of the oldest and most well-preserved structures of the ancient world. It was built as a tomb for the Egyptian pharaoh **Khufu** (also known as "Cheops" in English), who ruled during the 26th Dynasty of Egypt, around 2580 BCE.\n\n', '</think>\n\n</think>\n\nSure! Here are a few more thoughts on why chewing sugarless gum might not be the best choice for everyone:\n\n### 1. **Potential Side Effects**:\n   - **Gag Reflex**: Some people experience a gag reflex when they chew gum, especially if the gum is too sweet or sticky.\n   - **Dry Mouth**: Sugarless gum can sometimes cause dry mouth, which can lead to discomfort, especially when eating or drinking.\n   - **Increased Sugar Intake**: While the gum itself is sugar-free, some people might accidentally consume sugar from other sources (like candies or drinks)