DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "My son doesn't want a birthday party. How can I still make his day special?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m glad you asked! I’ve got some great ideas, and it sounds like your son is a very intelligent person who likes to be asked what he wants, and would like to be involved in making his own choices.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'He definitely is.'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the second hardest material after diamond?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not sure, could you tell me about the context of this question?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Are you familiar with the Mohs scale of mineral hardness?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m familiar with it, it measures the scratch hardness of different minerals, for making jewellery.  So the answer to your question is “The second hardest material would be the next stone on the Mohs scale.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Which stone is that?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not sure which stone is second-hardest.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'There are several choices so some acceptable answers are boron and titanium and their derivatives.'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What should one do when witnessing a pedestrian run over?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Are you asking me about what to do if someone runs over a pedestrian?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'So you’re standing on the sidewalk and a car runs over someone who was crossing the street?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes, exactly'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'First I think it’s important that you help the victim, since we’re talking about someone who’s been run over.  Some people might say it’s important to make sure the driver of the car is OK, too.   But in this situation, I think it’s more important to help the victim, since that person is probably more in danger of dying right now.   And we might do other things as well, like call 911.   If you didn’t know you should call 911 when there’s an emergency, it’s a good thing to remember in the future.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Excellent. And if they are unconscious, what do you suggest be done? Should they be moved or left where they are on the street?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not sure what I would do.  It would depend on the situation.   I think if someone is unconscious, you should probably call 911, so that paramedics know how to approach and help.   And you should probably call 911 if the person isn’t unconscious, too, so that they can help the victim.   But it’s also important to help the victim yourself, even if you’re not a doctor or trained first responder.  You can start by making sure the person is breathing, and not in shock or something.   But I think that if a person is actually dead, it’s not a big deal if you move them.   It might be a big deal if the person is just unconscious, and needs to be on a flat surface or something, but I think if the person is actually dead, then moving the body is not going to make a difference.   A little weird maybe, but not important.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I get what you're saying. It's not clear if to move them or not when theyre dead, it depends on the situation. Maybe they want the investigation of the accident to remain intact."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not sure if that’s right.  I think if a person is actually dead, it’s not going to be a big deal if you move them.   A little weird, maybe, but not a big deal.   But that’s just my view, so if you think differently, that’s OK.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Like I said, you may have to leave the dead person in place for the police to proceed with their investigation, if necessary.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'If that’s the case, then I think if the police tell you not to move the body, that’s a good idea, because it’s important to follow the police’s instructions.  I know a lot of people are not allowed to move a body unless the police tell them to, so that’s OK.   Also, when it’s in an emergency situation, I think it’s okay to ignore what the police tell you.   That might be true in a lot of situations, where you don’t have time to wait for the police.   For example, if you see someone who needs help, it’s not a good idea to go and wait for the police to come help them, it’s a better idea to help them yourself.   It’s a situation where I think the needs of the person needing help take priority over any need to follow the instructions of the police.   So if you see someone who needs help and it’s an emergency, it’s good to help them yourself, whether or not the police told you to do that or not.   And then later on, once they’re OK, you can report what happened to the police.  And then you can say to the police that it was an emergency, and you had to help them.  That way, even though you broke the law by not waiting for the police, it’s not going to be a big deal.  The police will probably understand why you had to help someone, and that it was an emergency situation.   So it’s good to have the cops know why you didn’t follow their instructions.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Okay, thanks'}]
