DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Are asteroids a legitimate danger to humans on planet earth?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Gosh, this is a really difficult question!  While many asteroids are “near-earth”, there are still a lot that pass really far away, and we’re still discovering new ones every year.  I’d say that earth is safe for the foreseeable future, but let’s talk about this together.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "D we have technology available to us should we locate an asteroid that's on a collision course with earth?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Sure, we can talk about that.  Most asteroids are really small, so actually there are only a handful of asteroids that are larger than a kilometer across, and they don’t come very close to us.  But there are plenty of asteroids in the “ten-meter” class and even smaller.  Let’s say that an asteroid that size hits us - the energy of the collision would be equivalent to detonating several hundred nuclear warheads.  That would be a massive disaster, with people everywhere panicking, and much loss of life and property.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What was the last meteor that could have possibly damaged life on earth?'}]
