DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How can I help to make an adopted child feel more welcome in a new home?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'A key thing to do, I think, is to learn more about the child’s life prior to coming into your home, and how it’s different from what they’re used to now.  If you don’t already know, try asking the child about what their favorite things were in their past home, what they miss from before, and what they’re most happy about now.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How long does it usually take before I can expect them to be comfortable in the home?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m not sure, as it depends a lot on the child and their family, and the kinds of issues that they have.  But I think in general it is about a month, or two months, or something like that.  It is also important to think about things on a timeline of about a year or two.  So, thinking about how the child will feel about moving to another family someday is a good thing to think about.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Do you know of any other things that I should try to avoid doing that would make them uncomfortable?'}]
