DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "I've had a macbook for 10 months and the battery capacity is already down to 85%. What should I do to check whether this is normal or not?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think there are a few different things you might want to ask about here. The easiest thing to start with might be to just check whether what you’re describing is normal for this model of laptop. That’s also really quick and easy, just do a google search or ask in the Apple Genius forums. If it’s abnormal, you might want to move on to asking about whether that’s because of something you’ve done to it, or because of a fault. If you think it’s something you’ve done, try asking on a forum about that particular thing you’ve done, or ask in the apple forum. But if you think there’s a fault, you should go ahead and get it serviced, which will probably cost a lot. If it’s still under warranty, you’ll be able to ask the Apple Genius to diagnose it and recommend a fix, but if it’s not under warranty you’ll have to do that yourself.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Apple tells me unless the battery health goes below 80%, they won't replace it and it doesn't come under warranty. However, I've heard this is very unusual battery depletion."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I can’t speak for Apple, but I think this is quite a clear signal that this laptop should be replaced, and it should absolutely be under warranty. I don’t really want to speak on behalf of Apple here, but based on the things you’ve said, I don’t really see how they could justifiably deny your warranty claim, as long as you’re being honest and they verify that the battery is indeed defective. But I want to be clear: it sounds like you’ve already asked Apple, and they won’t replace it because the battery health is still over 80%. So maybe you’re fine with it now, but just to give you a heads up: if they’re going to stand by their decision to deny your warranty claim, that’s a sign that you should consider not paying them for this laptop, and simply getting a new one from another company instead.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Is there a way for me to argue with Apple when they tell me this isn't covered by warranty?"}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "Are there any states that it's legal to own a Racoon as a pet?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Yes, there are many places where it's legal to own raccoons, and also other kinds of wild animals as pets."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you list a couple states I could own a pet racoon?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'In the United States, the following states allow the ownership of wild animals as pets: Alabama, Alaska, Arizona, Arkansas, Colorado, Idaho, Illinois, Iowa, Kansas, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, Montana, Nebraska, New Mexico, North Carolina, North Dakota, Ohio, Oklahoma, South Carolina, South Dakota, Tennessee, Utah, West Virginia, and Wisconsin.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What kind of food would I need to buy for raising a pet racoon?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What should I have for breakfast'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'A healthy breakfast can help you stay energized throughout the day.  It’s important that you don’t eat too much, and eat enough protein to fill you up.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What do you recommend?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'To find a healthy breakfast, you might want to consider:  a bowl of oatmeal;  a smoothie or yogurt with fruit; a toasted whole-grain English muffin with peanut butter or jam;  a cereal with low-fat milk or low-fat yogurt.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How important is eating breakfast?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Healthy breakfasts are very important, because they give you the energy you need to start the day off well.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'If I want to lose weight should I skip breakfast?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you give some pointers on writing a professional college email?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Of course. Let’s say you want to send an email to someone at your college. First of all, what sort of email are you sending? A “proposal”, or a “question”, or a “meeting request”, etc?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'A meeting request'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Then you’re going to want to make sure you write it in the proper format for the department, and include the appropriate subject line, format, and length.  For a meeting request, you want to be sure you’re taking an active role in the meeting, rather than just “making a request”, or suggesting that someone should contact you.  You also need to frame the request appropriately for the given department.  For example, in many departments the letters might need to go through the department chair, in which case you’d need to contact that person first.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Okay. Say you wanna meet with the chair of the chemistry department.'}]
