Initial completions: ["Holistic medicine is a medical practice that emphasizes the importance of treating the whole person, rather than just the disease or symptom. It considers the physical, emotional, mental, and spiritual aspects of a patient's health. This approach seeks to promote overall wellness by addressing the root causes of illness and fostering a balance between the body, mind, and spirit.\n\nTo practice holistic medicine, healthcare providers may use a variety of approaches, including acupuncture, herbal medicine, yoga, meditation, and nutritional counseling. These practices aim to enhance the body's natural healing abilities and support the patient's overall well-being.\n\nIt is important to note that holistic medicine is not a substitute for conventional medical care. It is often used in conjunction with traditional medical treatments to provide a more comprehensive approach to patient care. Some holistic practitioners may also offer alternative therapies, such as massage therapy or energy healing, as part of their practice.\n\nHowever, it is also important to recognize that holistic medicine is not universally accepted, and its effectiveness can vary.", "</think>\n\nAbsolutely! **Eba** is a beloved Nigerian dish that's incredibly rich in flavor and has a deep cultural significance. It's a great way to introduce your guests to the vibrant flavors of Nigerian cuisine. Here's a **simple and delicious recipe for Eba** that you can make at home:\n\n---\n\n### 🍽 **Eba (Nigerian Bitter) Recipe**\n\n#### 🍳 Servings: 4–6 people\n#### 🥦 Time: 45 minutes\n\n---\n\n#### 🥣 Ingredients:\n\n- 1 cup (200 ml) rice (basmati or any type)\n- 1 cup (200 ml) water\n- ½ cup (100 ml) oil (vegetable or peanut)\n- 2 tbsp (30 g) salt\n- 2 tbsp (30 g) sugar\n- 1 tbsp (15 g) ground nutmeg (optional, for extra flavor)\n-", "</think>\n\nThat's a great and thoughtful approach! It's absolutely possible to be in a serious and long-term relationship while also working on yourself. In 