DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I clean my gutters'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You can clean your gutters using a few different methods. First, you should clean them outside in the rain as a method of self-cleaning. Second, if you are using the gutters as a drain, you should use some good soapy water or a few drops of dish soap to clean them. Third, you should consider using a long, stiff brush with a little water on the end to clean the clogs that have accumulated in your gutters.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is is safe to use soap in gutters'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'It is fine to use soap, but it is not necessary. Soap will not leave harmful chemicals behind and will not cause harm.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I keep the gutter from clogging'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You can keep the gutter from clogging by making sure that the gutter is not blocked. There should be no leaves, sticks, or other debris stuck in the gutter. If the gutter is clogged, you should clean it. You can use soap and water to clean it, and you can use a brush to remove debris.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How long will that take'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What should I do when escaping a fire in a house?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'This is a really common scenario. I’d recommend making sure the smoke and fire alarm isn’t being triggered, and evacuating from the basement (this is probably the safest place), to the main floor (and avoid rooms with potential for a fire to spread to, like the kitchen or master bedroom). Avoid walking through smoke or thick dust, and avoid open flames, so as to not ignite clothing or hair. For safety, wear protective equipment like masks, and wet/layered clothing. For re-entry to the house, stay on the perimeter of the scene, and avoid touching any fire or smoke. If the fire is not under control, leave the scene, and notify emergency services (911, in the US) as quickly as possible. If an extinguisher is available, do not throw water onto the fire. Instead, douse it in an area where it can’t spread, or use a foam extinguisher to smother the fire. Otherwise, extinguishers should be held with both hands, and aimed at the base of the fire. After evacuating the building, avoid all windows and doors, since smoke, heat, and debris could blow out of the building (in the unlikely event of an explosion). For other'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I don't want to re-enter the house after getting out safely."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Alright, well the best course of action is to get out of the house immediately. It’s not safe to re-enter the building, and even if you stay outside of the area of the fire, that smoke is dangerous and can cause breathing problems.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How can I avoid smoke? Maybe stay low to the ground if the smoke is heavy?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'It’s good to be aware of smoke and avoid moving around too much.  You can cover your nose and mouth, and stay low to the ground if it’s heavy.  It’s best to not re-enter the building, since the smoke is dangerous.  There is no particular way to know which direction the smoke is coming from.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Thanks I'll be able to make a fire escape plan with all of this information."}]
