DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you help me learn how to convert Fahrenheit to Celsius?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The math formula is:'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can you walk me through an example? It is 37 degrees Fahrenheit right now, what is that in Celsius?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': '37 degrees Fahrenheit = 38 degrees Celsius, so the equation is C = F - 32 x 5/9. 37 - 32 = 5, so 5 x 5/9 = 2.5, so we say C = F - 32 x 5/9 = F - 32 x 2.5 = F - 65.75.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "This doesn't seem right at all. What is 37 degrees Fahrenheit when converted to Celsius?"}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I become a good storyteller?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I’m very pleased that you want to become a storyteller!  You could start with your experiences in life, and then see if you can connect them to stories about other people or situations.  If you have a child who’s going through a difficult time, you might see that as a challenge or a story.  Or maybe you saw a movie that made an impression on you, or read a book that made you feel moved.  Once you have a beginning, then you can try thinking of details to support the main story and make it more realistic.  You can also make these connections in a way that relates to you, so you’re not telling your whole life story.  Maybe there’s a specific character or problem you want to tell the story around?  If you get stuck at a point, you could ask me to help, and then see if you can’t come up with more of the story.  Stories need to be believable in some way, even if they are hard to believe.  So I think the story that you want to tell must have a reason to be told.  We should also take care to make sure it’s not something people have heard before, or are telling right now,'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I mean an oral storyteller.'}]
