DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How are typhoons formed?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'They’re formed when the ocean heats up in a spiral pattern.  When the water temperature goes over 86°F, the ocean is too warm for there to be as many clouds or thunderstorms as there normally are.  So a lot of water evaporates and turns into a hot, rising layer of air, which cools down once it gets into the upper atmosphere.  That cooling process creates a lot of condensation, which forms clouds, which in turn create rain and snow.  And rain can cause runoff, which causes the ocean to rise and lower, which creates stronger winds and can lead to flooding.  When the winds grow strong enough, they can reach hurricane strength, even if the ocean temperature hasn’t increased all that much.  The hurricane then grows to a typhoon.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "That's scary stuff. Where do typhoons get their fast-moving winds?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'We don’t know much about the inner workings of hurricanes or typhoons, but it is hypothesized that the temperature difference from cold to warm water (called thermal gradient) can produce strong winds.  Air can be warm or cold when it rises up from the ocean or falls down towards it, and so the updraft or downdraft can become a strong wind and cause rotation.  That rotation is thought to create the strong winds of hurricanes and typhoons.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I see. How then does a typhoon travel from one point to another?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'They move by getting hit with winds that push them in that direction.  When they get really strong, these winds can have the power to create waves that can even reach up to 30 feet high.  Once a typhoon travels far enough from the ocean, the winds and waves die down because there isn’t much water to continue to act as a pressure source.  But we still sometimes get big waves because of the strong winds and the swirling ocean currents.  And the tornadoes and hailstorms that form in strong winds can often travel hundreds of miles.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Why does a typhoon move so slow?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How can I act as santa claus?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'If you mean how can you act in a way that will make people think you are a Santa Claus, the best way would be to stand in front of a large crowd of people and deliver a very concise and inspiring Christmas message, and keep repeating it for several days. You could take someone’s picture with your smartphone when you do this, to prove it really happened.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I dress like santa?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "I've heard of Reddit, but I don't know what it is. Can you explain it?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Reddit is an online community centered around the sharing of user-submitted links to content that its users think is interesting or funny.  You can browse posts that have been voted up to the top, or submitted by users that you follow.  For example, if you use Reddit, you can go to http://reddit.com/, and then visit a subreddit you\'re interested in, like "/r/dailyinterview/" or "/r/aww/" and browse posts from other users that you follow.  People can post anything that interests them to their accounts, including links, pictures, articles, videos, etc.  You can also subscribe to specific subreddits, to see new posts from users you follow that are posted in that subreddit.  For example, I can subscribe to your subreddit "/r/aww/", and you can see my posts if you click on the "following" button on the top right corner of your page, and then click on "Public subscriptions" in the menu that comes up.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is it free to join Reddit?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The site is free to use, but you can set up a free account to share posts publicly.  If you sign up for a Reddit account, you\'ll be able to follow and comment on specific users\' posts, and you\'ll also have an "inbox" where you can be emailed whenever someone sends you a private message.  If you want to also post and comment on threads without your own account, you can do that by using the "reddit.com" website.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I choose a username for Reddit?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You can change your username whenever you want, but you may want to pick one that\'s relatively unique, so you don\'t run into any trouble with people using it.  I recommend trying out some possible names in the website\'s "username suggestions" box before you finalize your choice.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Great. How does karma work on Reddit?'}]
