DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What can you see in Rome?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Great question!  A lot has been built there over thousands of years, and all of it is still there, so it makes for an interesting and instructive visit!  We can start with the Colosseum, which was built in the second century AD by the Roman emperor Vespasian. It was a great spectator event for more than one hundred years, with up to fifty thousand spectators watching people fight, and more recently it has been the scene of many important cultural events. We can also see the Pantheon, which was built in the second century AD, and was converted into a church by the Roman emperor Hadrian, and is now a World Heritage site.  I think my favorite thing to do in Rome is to go to the Forum Romanum, which was once the center of life for the Roman Empire, and is one of the greatest ruins of antiquity still standing.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Great information.  I want to go to an opera too.  Can you suggest where I should go and whom to see?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Oh, so glad you asked!  Do you have a favorite style of opera?  Do you prefer highbrow, comic, tragic, dramatic, or comedic?  And who are your favorite performers?  There are many options here: you could go to an opera at the Metropolitan Opera, a popular Italian opera, or go to Teatro alla Scala in Milan, Italy, where famous Italian performers like Pavarotti and Cavaradossi performed.  You might also enjoy a performance at the Paris Opera, if you’re ever in France!  Or you could go to the Venice Opera, or see the Bolshoi Ballet.  Opera is a worldwide art form, so there is something for everyone!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Comedy will be preferable.'}]
