DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I get my dog to stop excessive licking?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Hm, that’s a pretty tricky one, I have to say.  My best guess would be to maybe try to distract him with a new toy or something, maybe?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'My dog licks everything and does not stop when I tell him to or when I scold him. What do you suggest?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Well it sounds like you’re both enjoying yourselves.  What’s important to keep in mind here is that how your dog responds to the world is how he learns about it.  So if he’s getting rewarded for his behavior, it makes sense that he’s continuing to do it.  If you want him to do something differently, I suggest redirecting his attention to something else.  In general it sounds like he’s enjoying himself.  If you want to do something about that licking behavior, I suggest finding a dog therapist in your area that can help with behavioral issues.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'So far, I have tried distracting my dog with his favorite ball to disrupt the licking behavior. The distraction helps for a little while but does not correct the problem.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'How is the ball working?  Does it distract him from the licking behavior for long?  Did it teach him to associate the ball with the distraction?  It’s possible he might just become more motivated to lick in the face of being distracted.  I do think the goal of being distracted is a good one though.  Perhaps you could try playing with the ball or practicing some commands with him and then redirect him away from licking.  That way, he might start to associate licking with playing ball, and you can then ask him to stop playing ball so he can sit.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I understand dogs lick things to understand what things are in the world and to determine if they are good, or bad. Once, my dog had licked a fan while it was running and decided never to lick a fan again. He learned that he could get hurt by the fan.'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I read something that stated "humans can only see  0.0035 percent of the visible light spectrum". What is hypothesized as making up the portions that humans cannot see?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'If I understand correctly, the current state of understanding is that we only perceive a narrow portion of the visible spectrum of light. This idea goes back as far as Aristotle, who theorized about a limit on how many colors and shades we could perceive. Since that time, scientific thinking has continued to recognize this limitation.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Have there been any discoveries about what makes up the other part of this spectrum?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'If by "what makes up" you mean, "is there a particular chemical, object, or physical phenomenon that’s preventing humans from perceiving this area of light", then I do not know the answer to that. In general, what people study and recognize in terms of electromagnetic radiation is what gets to us on Earth after traveling through the atmosphere.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What do scientists hypothesize? Is "dark matter" related to this concept?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How often should I get a check up with my general doctor?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'I think regular checkups are a good idea.   It can be hard to know what is normal in your body, so it’s helpful to get a second opinion.   In particular, it’s important to get a check up if you’re concerned about changes in your health.  For example, if you experience pain or a feeling of illness.   The doctor will be able to listen to your heart, and look at your skin and inside your eyes, and make sure you’re healthy.   You’ll also have the opportunity to ask your doctor any questions you might have about your health, and ask what you can do to stay healthy in the future.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How often is regular?'}]
