DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'I need a really good rope for hauling a big piece of furniture. Would polyester be better than nylon, and if so, why is that? What are their differences?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Polyester has some properties that make it a better rope for some purposes, but nylon has others that make it a better rope for other purposes.  One property of polyester that I think is relevant is that it’s “relatively stiff and not easily deformable” which is useful for holding a lot of weight, since it reduces the chances that the rope will stretch or break under that kind of force.  Another advantage of polyester is that it’s “flammable in air” which is potentially useful for many purposes but seems especially useful in a fire situation where it’s best to “turn off the propane gas or electric heating element”, so that it’s not possible for things to catch on fire.  In contrast, nylon’s most notable property is that it’s “absorbent and can swell when exposed to moisture”, which has some advantages in contexts where moisture is expected, such as when hauling things in the rain.  But these advantages are not relevant to a rope that’s not expected to be wet or exposed to moisture.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Does its stiffness make polyester less able to stretch?'}]
