DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do you make homemade soap bar?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'You can purchase a bar of soap (called a “soap bar”) from a grocery store or natural foods store, and then melt that soap bar in a double boiler or a saucepan.  The soap will melt, and you can then pour that soap into molds.  You can pour the melted soap into either silicone soap molds, which are cheap and reusable, or fancy soap molds that will last for decades.  You can also buy soap in a “soap base” instead of as a bar, and it’s easier to get the right consistency and form in this case.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'No, I mean making it from scratch. Your method is just remolding an already made bar of soap.'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How come people say not to put hot food in the fridge?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'That’s an interesting question! I think it’s mostly a convention, and then maybe also practical. I can tell you the “conventional” reason, and I could tell you what would happen if you put it in, but I can’t tell you if this is actually the best option for you.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Well, what are the reasons?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'When food is hot, its moisture is released in the form of steam. The warm water is rising inside the container, so the liquid is more dense than the air, and so if you seal the container there will be some pressure inside it, like a party balloon. If you put the container inside the refrigerator, the air inside the fridge is chilled and so it is more dense than the steam inside the container. If you let the pressure build up too much, the food will rupture the container and spill everywhere.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'That makes sense. Are there other reasons?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Sure. If you put hot food in the fridge it will likely drop to a low temperature and not be good for much longer. It’s also harder to keep the temperature low in the fridge, because the fridge and the food are in direct contact and there’s less insulation. So you might warm up the other food in the fridge, or the refrigerator could overheat and burn out, etc.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I'm not sure what you mean that it will likely drop to a low temperature and not be good for much longer. I mean, if I decide I'm gonna eat the rest of my soup tomorrow and I want to put it in the fridge til then, why would it being hot when I put it in make it 'not be good for much longer'?"}]
