DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'How do I clean a coffee maker?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The two options for cleaning a coffee maker are a wash cycle, and a soak cycle. For each, the coffee maker comes out of the machine. For the wash cycle, you start by spraying the inside of the coffee maker with some liquid dish soap and water, then run a cycle of hot water through the machine until all the dishes and utensils are clean.  Then you need to dry the inside of the machine, and clean the counter tops.  For the soak cycle, you start by spraying the inside of the coffee maker with some liquid dish soap and water, then run a cycle of hot water through the machine until all the dishes and utensils are clean.  Then you need to remove all the dishes and utensils from the machine, and allow the water to soak for ten to thirty minutes.  Then you need to wipe the inside of the machine, and dry the inside.  After that you can clean the counter tops.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Can I use vinegar to run through the machine?'}]
DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "What is the end of the phrase 'when life gives you lemons..'?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The phrase has been used in a variety of contexts, including in a popular song and in a movie.  The most common form of the saying is attributed to a 12th-century Jewish rabbi who was having a particularly bad day.  Apparently he lamented how there was no medicine or other remedy for his troubles, saying “Go and learn a lesson from my troubles: when life gives you lemons, make lemonade.”  Many people have changed the original words to their own ends, like adding a word like “be” or “and” or “or” before “make”.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Is that an example of a metaphor or an idiom?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Yes, it’s a metaphor, where you take a known situation (life, a lemon, and perhaps a glass of lemonade) and then replace the things that are known with other words or phrases that have a similar but distinct meaning.  In this case the replacement is new circumstances and a recipe for a lemonade.  The way that new meaning is derived from the initial situation is metaphorically, or imagistically, not literally, which is what distinguishes it from an idiom.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Could you use it if a situation either good or bad?'}]
