DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What\'s the origin of the phrase "to get ones goat"?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "It's a popular joke about a cowboy. The idea is that if you “get a man's goat” you'll annoy him and he'll be extremely mad at you, because the cow's “goat” is a symbol of cattle and cowboys."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "I don't think this is true. Can you give me another explanation?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'It might be a folk etymology.  The original meaning of "goat" is "an obnoxious person".  The cow\'s "goat" is not a symbol of cattle or cowboys, it\'s a symbol of any obnoxious person.  Folk etymology was invented to explain the origins of various puns and jokes.  There\'s probably a version of the "get ones goat" joke that involves an actual goat that makes more sense.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "That's still wrong. You get one more try. Why don't you start over and not refer to cowboys?"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'We’re playing a word game.  I’ll tell you what “get ones goat” means and then you tell me why I’m wrong.  Okay?  Let’s try it.  The meaning of “get ones goat” is “to annoy a person”, or “to irritate or infuriate a person”.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "Yes, that's the meaning I'm referring to. But what I want is the etymology!"}]
