DIALOGUE PAIRS: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the best bait to use when fishing for trout?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Depends on where you are.  Generally speaking trout have a taste for wild celery, or vegetation from the surrounding area.  But if you don’t want to use wild-grown plants, you could also try salmon roe, cheese, and worms.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'I am located on the east coast—fishing in a lake.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'It’s also important to choose what type of fish you’re trying to catch, since some fish require different bait.  I’m not sure what type of trout live in lakes, so this might be worth looking into.  But if you’re trying for brook trout, I’d recommend eating the native vegetation in the lake, since they seem to be partial to wild celery.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'The lake is stocked. Wild celery?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Ah, I think you may be misinterpreting the question.  Wild celery refers to vegetation that grows naturally in a lake, which is naturally available for the fish to eat.  “Stocked” usually refers to raising trout in a hatchery.  It’s a method of artificial fishing, to increase populations of the fish.  And it is possible that the fish you’re catching are hatchery-raised fish, not wild fish.  I’m not familiar with all the different types of trout, and I can’t be sure, but you might want to look into it.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Yes. That is correct.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Then I believe that you are misinterpreting what wild celery is.  Wild celery is a natural vegetation that grows in the lake, and it would not be raised in the hatchery.  So “wild celery” is not what you want.   But there are other options to bait fish.  What do you think about using worms?'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'No. I understand. I was unfamiliar with the term wild celery but you already clarified that for me earlier in the conversation. What are some baits??'}]
